from lib import *
S='social-science'
lesson(10,S,'economics-globalization-and-trade','stages-of-globalization-and-lpg',"Globalization: its history and the LPG reforms","A world that trades","The silk on a Roman lady, the spices in a Chinese kitchen and the phone in your hand all travelled along trade routes. Globalization is old, but its speed and reach have changed. This lesson follows it from the Silk Road to today's markets.",
["Globalization is the integration of a country with the world economy: a process of internationalization plus liberalization. @ definition",
 "Liberalization, Privatization and Globalization, together called LPG, are much talked about by politicians, economists and businessmen. @ LPG",
 "The term globalization was introduced by Prof. Theodore Levitt, and its history is discussed in three stages. @ three stages",
 "Archaic globalization: trade links between Sumer and the Indus Valley in the third millennium BC, and later the Silk Road linking the Roman, Parthian and Han empires. @ archaic stage",
 "Proto globalization: the rise of maritime European empires in the 16th and 17th centuries, first Portuguese and Spanish, then Dutch and British. @ proto stage",
 "Modern globalization: a higher share of trade in merchandise production, growth of trade in services, and production and trade by multinational firms in the 20th century. @ modern stage",
 "South Indian merchant guilds were formed to organise and expand trade, showing that trade with the world is old in the Tamil country too. @ South Indian guilds"],
("Is globalization good or bad for a small farmer or a small shop owner?",[("It opens bigger markets and cheaper goods for some people.","Benefits of wider trade."),("But small producers may face competition from large multinational firms.","Not everyone gains equally."),("Government policy and protection of small producers decide how fair it is.","Policy matters.")]),
("Describe the three stages of globalization with one example each.",["Archaic: Silk Road","Proto: European maritime empires","Modern: multinational firms and services"],["Thinking globalization started only after 1990"]),
["Globalization = integration of a country with the world economy.","LPG = Liberalization, Privatization, Globalization.","Three stages: archaic, proto, modern.","South Indian guilds show old trade links."],
["m|The term globalization was introduced by|Prof. Theodore Levitt|Adam Smith~Wrote on wealth of nations;Karl Marx~Not him|Levitt used the term.",
 "t|The Silk Road is an example of archaic globalization.|True|It linked the Roman, Parthian and Han empires.",
 "n|Number of stages of globalization discussed in the text|3|Archaic, proto and modern.",
 "m|LPG stands for|Liberalization, Privatization and Globalization|Land, People and Government~Not economics terms;Loans, Prices and Growth~Not the LPG reforms|These are the three economic reforms.",
 "t|Proto globalization was marked by the rise of maritime European empires.|True|In the 16th and 17th centuries.",
 "s|The first European maritime empires in proto globalization were the Portuguese and ____.|Spanish|Followed by the Dutch and British.",
 "t|Globalization means only the sale of goods inside one country.|False|It is integration with the world economy.",
 "m|Modern globalization is characterised by|growth of services and multinational firms|isolated villages~Opposite|barter only~Not modern|Trade in services grew."])
lesson(10,S,'economics-government-and-taxes','role-of-government-and-types-of-tax',"The role of government and taxes","Who pays for the road?","The road to your school, the police station, the ration shop: someone has to pay for them. Governments collect taxes and spend them on services for everyone. This lesson explains what governments do and how taxes work.",
["The government's roles include defence, foreign policy, conduct of periodic elections, law and order, and public administration and provision of public goods. @ roles of government",
 "Local governments provide civic facilities like local roads, drainage, drinking water and waste collection and disposal. @ local governments",
 "Governments also redistribute income and reduce poverty by taxing higher incomes more and giving the very poor food, shelter, education, healthcare and income support. @ redistribution",
 "Tax is levied by the government for the development of the state's economy; the word comes from taxation, which means an estimate. @ tax",
 "Direct taxes are levied on the income of persons, while indirect taxes are levied on goods and services. @ direct and indirect",
 "The revenue of the government depends on direct and indirect taxes, which are its financial sources. @ revenue",
 "The Union government has a judicial system to settle disputes, and the state governments administer the police. @ law and order"],
("Why do high-income people pay more tax than the poor, and where does the money go?",[("A tax system that takes more from the rich can fund help for the poor.","Redistribution."),("The money funds defence, roads, schools and hospitals.","Public goods."),("Direct taxes fall on income; indirect taxes are paid when we buy goods.","Two types.")]),
("Differentiate between direct and indirect taxes with an example of each.",["Direct tax on income, for example income tax","Indirect tax on goods and services, for example GST","Taxes pay for public goods"],["Thinking only rich people pay indirect taxes"]),
["Governments provide defence, law and order, public goods and local services.","Direct tax is on income; indirect tax is on goods and services.","Taxes also help to reduce poverty and inequality."],
["m|A tax levied on the income of a person is|a direct tax|an indirect tax~That is on goods and services;a loan~Not a tax|Income tax is an example.",
 "t|Indirect taxes are levied on goods and services.|True|For example GST.",
 "m|Which is a function of local governments?|waste collection and drinking water|defence of the country~Union function;foreign policy~Union function|Civic facilities.",
 "t|The government has no role in reducing poverty.|False|It redistributes income and supports the poor.",
 "s|The word tax comes from taxation, which means an ____.|estimate|It originally meant an assessment.",
 "t|State governments administer the police force in their states.|True|The Union has a vibrant judicial system.",
 "m|The Union government is responsible for|creating and maintaining defence forces|running village schools alone~Mostly local;collecting all local taxes~Not so|Defence is a Union duty.",
 "t|Periodic elections are part of the government's role in a democracy.|True|Elections to Parliament, assemblies and local bodies."])
lesson(10,S,'economics-food-security-and-nutrition','availability-access-and-absorption',"Food security and nutrition security","Enough food, every day","A full granary does not feed a hungry family if they cannot afford the grain or cannot digest it safely. Food security has three parts: availability, access and absorption.",
["Food security exists when all people, at all times, have physical, social and economic access to sufficient, safe and nutritious food (FAO, 2009). @ FAO definition",
 "Dr M.S. Swaminathan said nutrition security means access to a balanced diet, safe drinking water, environmental hygiene, primary health care and primary education. @ Swaminathan",
 "Availability is the physical presence of food stocks, depending on domestic production, changes in stocks and imports. @ availability",
 "Access is mainly purchasing power, linked to employment, assets and education. @ access",
 "Absorption is the ability to biologically utilise the food eaten, which needs health and hygiene. @ absorption",
 "Food grain production rose with HYV seeds of rice and wheat: from about 54 million tonnes in the early 1950s on a little over 98 million hectares, with an average yield of 547 kg per hectare. @ food grains",
 "So food security depends both on the quantity of food and on people's ability to buy it and stay healthy. @ more than production"],
("A country has record grain stocks but many poor families go hungry. Which component of food security is failing?",[("Availability is fine because stocks are high.","Stocks exist."),("Access fails because the poor lack money to buy grain.","Purchasing power."),("So production alone cannot ensure food security.","Need jobs and income too.")]),
("Explain the three components of food and nutrition security.",["Availability","Access","Absorption"],["Equating food security only with production"]),
["Three components: availability, access, absorption.","Access depends on income and employment.","HYV seeds raised grain production after the 1950s.","Nutrition security also needs water, hygiene and health care."],
["m|Physical presence of food stocks is called|availability|access~Purchasing power|absorption~Biological use|It depends on production, stocks, imports.",
 "t|Access to food is mainly a matter of purchasing power.|True|Linked to employment and assets.",
 "n|The number of basic components of food and nutrition security|3|Availability, access and absorption.",
 "m|Absorption of food refers to|biological utilisation of the food eaten|storing grain in godowns~That is availability|buying food in markets~That is access|Needs health and hygiene.",
 "t|High production alone guarantees food security.|False|People must also be able to afford food.",
 "s|The scientist who defined nutrition security in the text is Dr M.S. ____.|Swaminathan|Eminent agricultural scientist.",
 "n|Average yield of food grains in the early 1950s (kg per hectare)|547|About 54 million tonnes on 98 million hectares.",
 "m|HYV stands for|high yielding varieties|heavy yield vegetables~Not meant|home grown varieties~Not meant|HYV seeds raised yields."])
